  THE SCORE IS GEOMETRY. At every k the creature's per-axis accuracy is
  within 2 SE of what its own DELIVERED spacing and scatter predict, so
  the falling excess is not a readout failure and `nearest` is behaving.
  The question is what shrinks the DELIVERY, and F1 answers it cleanly
  because its demand is 59 Hz in all three arms.

  MORE WORDS COST F1 REACH AND COST NAMING NOTHING. The two results that
  clear the 3 SE bar, paired within creature: F1 delivery falls -11.8
  +/- 3.5 Hz (3.3 SE) from six words to nine at matched teaching, and
  the TOTAL naming excess is conserved (-0.012 +/- 0.019, 0.6 SE). So the
  creature gives up F1 separation without naming any worse.

  THE MECHANISM IS NOT ESTABLISHED. The obvious reading -- F2 absorbs
  what F1 gives up -- does NOT clear the bar and must not be written as
  if it did:
    F2 side excess   +0.079 +/- 0.027 (2.9 SE)   UNDER the bar
    F2 delivered Hz  +1.8 +/- 13.1 (0.1 SE)   TOO NOISY TO READ
    F1 side excess   -0.026 +/- 0.026 (1.0 SE)   a null, not a value
  F2 is the WRONG AXIS to read this on by construction: it has 2 levels
  at k6 and 3 at k9+, so its two delivery estimates are not the same
  estimator. The arm that would settle it is a 2x3 grid -- six words
  with THREE F2 levels -- which holds the F2 estimator fixed and moves
  word count alone.
  The teaching budget is bounded, not excluded: k9+ - k9 is +0.1 +/- 3.5 Hz
  (0.0 SE, n=18), which at the 3 SE bar still admits 10.8 Hz.
